<p class='text'>From time to time people ask me if I'm being honest when I say I'm not afraid of dying.</p>
<p class='text'>I honestly think I am.  From time to time I even think I welcome it: the struggle is real and when you die the struggle is over.  And I do know that when I did nearly die from a lung abscess twenty or so years ago, I did not panic.  But the same thought occurred to me then that always occurs to me: what happens to the rest of the world without me?</p>
<p class='text'>Not, of course, the big picture world, which doesn't even really know I exist, but the small corner of it that does: my family, the people I work with, my neighbours, my landlord; and how does all the mess I've made - from bank accounts to github repositories - get tidied up?</p>
<p class='text'>I usually think about this in terms of wills - as in "last will and testament" - which increasingly seems a very outdated concept.  You write down your thoughts about once every decade, and that sort of fits the big picture of where you are at, but it doesn't deal with so many things, especially now that we all have such a huge electronic footprint.</p>
<p class='text'>It occurred to me this morning - and this is where I probably lose everyone who isn't a programmer - that it is a bit like trying to recover from an exception in a programming language like Java.  What you need is an exception handler or a "finally" clause.  You want to be able to set up a whole bunch of things that will happen when you die, but there isn't anywhere to put them.  And even if you did set up an automated process and told your executor "run this when I die", how would you ever debug it?  I mean, you wouldn't be around to, would you?</p>
<p class='text'>Yes, that's basically what worries me about dying - the mess that I leave behind that someone else has to clean up after me.  And the small hole I will leave in people's lives who knew me and enjoyed being around me.  And I wonder things like, "what will happen to this website"?  Will someone choose to keep paying to keep it up?  Will they know how?  What happens when software rot invariably sets in?  Does anybody want any of the personal software assets that I leave behind?</p>
<p class='text'>Yes, I feel guilty about all of that.  Mainly because I've made the mess and the one thing I know for sure is that it won't be me cleaning it up.</p>
